{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmbhBd5hCMW9TQzzGJad","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X8Lo86b1zUq4Xtg3rwKrAK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop; C=$D/iter_4/candidate_3_repair_1\ncp $D/models/streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip.py $C/candidate.py\npython3 - \"$C/candidate.py\" <<'PY'\nimport sys\np=sys.argv[1]; s=open(p).read()\nhdr='''\"\"\"Accelerating gambler's run-length belief in the streak-weary pseudo-flip judgment.\n\nRefinement of the incumbent `streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip` (described below).\nSingle change: the functional form of the gambler's run-length belief. In the\nincumbent the believed log-odds of switching rise linearly with the current\nrun's length beyond two flips, g * (L - 2). Here they rise quadratically,\ng * (L - 2) ** 2: a run of three barely raises the expectation of a switch, but\neach further flip raises it more than the last, so a streak of five or six is\njudged far more improbable under chance than one of three or four, and the\nfirst break of a long streak is rewarded much more than the incumbent allows.\nNo parameter is added. Addresses the critique that among pairs of length >= 6\nin which both sequences have at most two switches, people choose the\nmore-switching sequence more often than the incumbent predicts.\n\nDescription of the refined model follows.\n\n'''\nassert s.startswith('\"\"\"')\ns=hdr+s[3:]\nold=\"            gam += (run - 2) * (1.0 if x != y else -1.0)\"\nassert old in s\ns=s.replace(old,\"            gam += (run - 2) ** 2 * (1.0 if x != y else -1.0)\")\nold2='g_run = pm.Normal(\"g_run\", mu=0.0, sigma=0.3)'\nassert old2 in s\ns=s.replace(old2,'g_run = pm.Normal(\"g_run\", mu=0.0, sigma=0.15)')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\ncat > $C/model_name.txt <<'EOF'\naccelerating_streak_weary_chance\nEOF\ncat > $C/hypothesis.md <<'EOF'\nRefinement of the incumbent `streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip` (people judge a sequence random to the extent their own second-order, streak-weary picture of a fair coin explains it better than the regular generators they suspect — a switch-biased coin, a heads-favoured trick coin and a repeating motif with slips — with pseudo-flip-diluted per-flip evidence, heavy-tailed signed person sensitivity and a side habit). The single change is in how their gambler's fallacy grows with streak length: the expectation that chance must switch does not rise steadily with each extra flip of a run but accelerates, so a run of three barely seems overdue while a run of five or six feels sharply improbable for a fair coin, and the first break of a long streak makes a sequence look far more random than the incumbent's linear belief allows. This addresses the critique that among long pairs where both sequences have at most two switches, people choose the more-switching sequence more often than the incumbent predicts.\nEOF\ngrep -n \"run - 2\\|g_run =\" $C/candidate.py\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir $C --responses $D/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8151,"cache_read_input_tokens":91264,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8151,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9781b992-42b2-4370-8b89-979387810a37","uuid":"36714d81-4b98-496a-8ca2-3e5512a5a8fe","timestamp":"2026-10-06T20:16:55.288Z","request_id":"req_011CfmbhBC2uu6z1hT14pPS7"}
